                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "GAMEPLAY:\n\nHonestly not sure what game to compare it to. It's a third-person action/platformer that's heavily dependent on how you've oriented the camera. You can chain together a bunch of movement actions to get around (jumping, crouching, rolling, wall riding) and build up lots of speed and air time in the process. Messing around and doing tricks is half the fun.\n\nThere are two playable characters, and each is fairly distinct from the other. The first character is close ranged, can block, and wall ride. The other throws knives at medium range but lacks the mobility and defense of the other, making up for it with strange phasing tricks. Combat is basically a game of timing, aiming, and picking your battles. And pick you will because enemies seem to spawn forever. This makes exploring kind of rough and nigh impossible if you don't know what you're doing. \n\nGRAPHICS/PERFORMANCE:\n\nYou would never guess that it's an indie game made by one dude. Models look great, animation's decent, solid textures, and a giant pile of shaders. Runs perfectly smooth maxed out on my modest-by-today's-standards rig (FX 8320, GTX 970). Also, jiggle physics!\n\nVALUE:\nThe play time is kind of short, but there's reason enough to go through as another character, practice your skills, and farm XP in what I assume is a cleverly hidden \"survival\" mode. The initial release has only one chapter, but more are forthcoming.\nRight now the asking price for Reventa is about as much as a couple of convenience store sodas. Given that alone, it's kind of a no-brainer. Plus, Reventa won't rot your teeth and give you the diabeetus.\n\nPROS:\nLooks great\nSmooth, responsive control\nFast paced\nLots of fancy tricks\nCool boss fight\nInexpensive\n\nCONS: \nControl takes a while to get used to\nCombat can be repetitive\nIt's short (for right now)",
